{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on the most probable outcome of each lottery, defined as the outcome with the highest probability. For Lottery A, there are two outcomes: -1000 Euros with 50% probability and 0 Euros with 50% probability. Both outcomes have equal probability (50%), so there is no unique most probable outcome—both are equally probable. For Lottery B, the outcome is -500 Euros with 100% probability, so the most probable outcome is clearly -500 Euros. Since the reason compares the most favorable among the most probable outcomes, and in Lottery A the most favorable outcome among those with maximum probability is 0 Euros (vs. -1000 Euros), the reason would consider 0 Euros as the most probable and most favorable outcome for A. For B, it is -500 Euros. Comparing 0 (from A) and -500 (from B), the reason would prefer Lottery A, as it has the more favorable most probable outcome.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report is in German. Translation: 'Simple decision, since at the end of the lottery I will definitely still have money and not lose everything. I decided for A because I don't see why I should definitely lose something, and would rather take the risk of possibly losing more, but with a relatively high probability, lose nothing. Knowing that I will still have some money after the decision makes it easier to take the risk.' The individual emphasizes avoiding a certain loss and values the possibility of losing nothing (i.e., getting 0 Euros) with 50% probability in Lottery A. They contrast this with Lottery B, which guarantees a loss of 500 Euros. The justification centers on the psychological comfort of retaining some money and the preference for risk-taking when it offers a chance of no loss. The individual considers the certainty of loss in B as a negative and views the 50% chance of no loss in A as a key factor. They do not mention probabilities of other outcomes beyond this, nor do they discuss expected values or worst-case scenarios in depth. The core of their reasoning is the presence of a no-loss outcome under A and the aversion to a sure loss in B.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on identifying the most probable outcome in each lottery (i.e., the outcome with the highest probability) and comparing the most favorable among those. In Lottery A, both outcomes have 50% probability, so the most favorable one (-0 Euros) is taken as the representative. In B, -500 Euros is the only and thus most probable outcome. The reason would prefer A because 0 > -500. The individual in the verbal report explicitly highlights the possibility of losing nothing (0 Euros) in A and contrasts it with the certainty of losing 500 in B. They value the existence of a no-loss outcome in A and use the probability of that outcome (50%, described as 'relatively high') as a justification. Although they do not explicitly frame their reasoning as 'choosing based on the most probable outcome,' they are clearly focusing on the outcome with the highest probability in B (certain loss) and in A (one of two equally probable outcomes, favoring the positive one). The emphasis on avoiding a sure loss and preferring a chance to keep money aligns with treating the 0 Euro outcome in A as decision-relevant in a way comparable to the reason’s logic. While the individual does not articulate the rule explicitly, their focus on the highest-probability outcomes and the comparison of their favorability strongly suggests alignment with the reason. The reasoning is not based on expected value, regret, or worst-case avoidance, but on the presence and desirability of a high-probability no-loss outcome.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
}

{
